 # Pinhole Camera Ray Generation
 
-Also called: Camera Ray Casting, Perspective Ray Generation, PinholeRayTracer::castRays
+Also called: Camera Ray Casting, FOV Calculation, Field of View Calculation, Fixed Focal Length Ray Precomputation, Perspective Ray Generation, Pinhole Camera, PinholeRayTracer::castRays
 
 ## Overview
-Pinhole Camera Ray Generation computes the origin and direction vectors for rays corresponding to each pixel in an image plane, assuming an ideal pinhole camera model with no lens distortion or depth of field. It differs from its parent, [Camera Matrix Ray Generation](2172-camera-matrix-ray-generation.md), by explicitly constructing the view frustum geometry (basis vectors and view plane bounds) from high-level camera parameters (eye, look-at, up, FOV) rather than relying on a pre-computed 4x4 projection/view matrix. It serves as the foundational ray setup for basic ray tracing and volume rendering pipelines where explicit control over the view plane sampling is required.
+Pinhole Camera Ray Generation computes the origin and direction vectors for rays corresponding to each pixel on an image plane, assuming an ideal pinhole camera model with no lens distortion or depth of field [S2, S4, unofficial]. It explicitly constructs the view frustum geometry (basis vectors and view plane bounds) from high-level camera parameters (eye position, look-at point, up vector, and field of view) rather than relying on a pre-computed projection matrix [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The method generates rays by defining a local coordinate system at the camera origin, mapping pixel indices to coordinates on a virtual view plane, and constructing direction vectors from the eye position through these points. Two primary implementation styles are documented: a look-at based approach (common in GPU implementations) and a rotation-matrix based approach (common in C++/Eigen implementations).
+The method generates rays by defining a local coordinate system at the camera origin, mapping pixel indices to coordinates on a virtual view plane, and constructing direction vectors from the eye position through these points [S2, unofficial]. Two primary implementation styles are documented: a look-at based approach (common in GPU implementations) and a rotation-matrix based approach (common in C++/Eigen implementations) [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
