 ## Operating characteristics
 *   **Time Complexity:** $O(W \times H)$, where $W$ and $H$ are image dimensions. The computation per pixel is constant time (a few trigonometric functions, vector operations, and normalizations).
 *   **Memory Complexity:** $O(W \times H)$ to store the generated ray origins and directions.
-*   **Performance:** The look-at variant involves computing basis vectors once ($O(1)$) and then iterating pixels. The rotation matrix variant involves matrix-vector multiplication per pixel, which is slightly more expensive but allows for easier integration with existing camera transformation pipelines.
 *   **Strengths:** Simple to implement; provides explicit control over the view plane geometry; no dependency on complex projection matrix decompositions.
 *   **Limitations:** Does not account for lens effects (bokeh, distortion) without extension; assumes an ideal pinhole model which may not match real-world sensor data without calibration.
 
 ## Background
-The pinhole camera model is the fundamental geometric model for perspective projection in computer graphics and computer vision. It assumes a single point of projection (the pinhole) and an image plane behind it. The method of generating rays by sampling the field of view using equal division of tangent is a common approximation in ray tracers to ensure uniform angular sampling [S1, unofficial]. The look-at formulation using basis vectors is standard in educational ray tracers and differentiable rendering frameworks [S2, unofficial]. The depth of field extension is a standard addition for realistic image synthesis [S3, unofficial].
